WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.870
- [Instructor] We are told
that a sample of a compound

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:07.190
containing only carbon and hydrogen atoms

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.890
is completely combusted,

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.580
producing 5.65 grams of carbon dioxide

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.290
and 3.47 grams of H2O or water.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.290
What is the empirical
formula of the compound?

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.260
So pause this video

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.860
and see if you can work through that.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.260
All right, now let's just try
to make sure we understand

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:27.700
what's going on.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.710
They say that I have
some mystery compound.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.820
It only contains carbon and hydrogen,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.610
so it's going to have
some number of carbon,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.860
I'll call that x.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.160
Some number of hydrogens,
I'll call that y.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.800
We're going to put it in the
presence of molecular oxygen

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.560
and it's going to combust

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.220
and after it's combusted,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.220
I'm going to end up with some
carbon dioxide and some water.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.240
And what I just drew here,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.940
this is a chemical reaction
that I'm describing.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.330
I haven't balanced it.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.650
I could try to, even with the x's and y's,

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.400
but that's not the point of this video.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.430
The point of this video is
they tell us how many grams

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.310
of the carbon dioxide we have

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.680
and how many grams of the water we have,

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.890
they tells us that right over there.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.310
And so what we need to
do is say, all right,

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.100
from that, we can figure
out how many moles

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.520
of carbon dioxide we have,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.380
how many moles of water we have,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.350
and from that, we can
figure out how many moles

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.930
of carbon did we start with

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.450
and how many moles of
hydrogen did we start with?

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.230
And if we look at those ratios,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.780
then we can come up with
the empirical formula

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.320
of the compound.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.020
So just to start, because
I'm going to be thinking

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.010
about molar moles and molar masses

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.410
and the mass of a mole
of a molecule or an atom,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.510
let's just get the average atomic mass

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.570
for carbon, hydrogen, and
oxygen for us to work with.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.740
So I'll get out our handy periodic table.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.523
We can see hydrogen has an
average atomic mass of 1.008.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.960
Let me write that down.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.473
So we have hydrogen is at 1.008,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.850
and then we have carbon,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:15.313
and carbon is at 12.01.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.083
So carbon is at 12.01,

00:02:23.375 --> 00:02:24.380
and we could also think about them

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.630
in terms of molar masses.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.160
We could say this is gram per mole,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.650
grams per mole,

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.430
and then last, but not
least, we have oxygen

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.430
and then oxygen is at
16.00 grams per mole.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.090
It's the average atomic mass,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.590
but whether we can think
of that as molar mass,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.100
that number is molar mass.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.880
So oxygen

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:57.190
is at 16.00 grams per mole.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.370
And so now we can try to figure out

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:06.370
how many moles of C in
the product do we have?

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.930
So we can see that all of
the carbon in the product

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.274
is in the carbon dioxide
that's in the product,

00:03:14.274 --> 00:03:19.067
and so we have 5.65 grams of CO2.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.700
We can think about how
many moles of CO2 that is,

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.270
so times one mole

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:32.270
of CO2 for every how many grams of CO2.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.900
Well, we just have to think about,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.002
actually, let me just
put it right over here.

00:03:39.002 --> 00:03:39.835
CO2,

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.090
you're going to have, let's see,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.420
you have one carbon

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.860
and two oxygens.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.860
So it is going to be
12.01 plus two times 16.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:58.110
Two times 16.00 grams per mole,

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.330
and so let's see.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.000
This would get us to, this is 32

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.340
plus 12.01, so that is 44.01.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.790
And that's grams per mole,

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.150
but now we're thinking
about moles per gram,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:14.150
so it's going to be one over 44.01

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.820
and so if we did just this,

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.020
the grams of CO2 would
cancel the grams of CO2

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.440
and this would give us moles of CO2,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.360
but I care about moles
of carbon in the product.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.520
So how many moles of carbon are there

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.310
for every mole of CO2?

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:35.310
Well we know that we
have one mole of carbon

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.750
for every one mole of CO2.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.110
Every carbon dioxide molecule
has one carbon in it,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.320
and so what is this going to get us?

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.640
So we have 5.65

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.310
divided by, divided by 44.01,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.060
and then times one, so I don't
have to do anything there.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.210
That is equal to, I'll round
it to three digits here,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.043
so 0.128,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.930
so this is 0.128

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.550
and my units here are, let's see.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:14.760
The grams of carbon dioxide

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.170
cancel the grams of carbon dioxide.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.410
The moles of carbon
dioxide cancel the moles

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:19.600
of carbon dioxide,

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.670
so I am exactly where I want to be.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:25.400
This is how many moles
of carbon that I have.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.460
And you can do the dimensional analysis,

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.060
but it also makes
intuitive sense, hopefully.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.850
If this is how many grams
of carbon dioxide we have

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.910
and a mole of carbon dioxide
is going to have a mass

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.030
of 44.01 grams,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.320
well then 5.65 over this
is going to tell us how,

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.720
what fraction of a mole
we have of carbon dioxide

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.550
and then whatever that
number of moles we have

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:48.860
of carbon dioxide is gonna be the same

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.550
as the moles of carbon,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.710
'cause we have one atom of carbon

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.800
for every carbon dioxide molecule,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.950
so that all makes sense.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.090
And now let's do the same for hydrogen.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.550
So let's think about moles of hydrogen

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.140
in the product,

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.030
and it's going to be the same exercise.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.670
And if you're so inspired

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.010
and you didn't calculate
it in the beginning,

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.330
I encourage you to try to
do this part on your own.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:17.080
All right, so all of the
hydrogen is in the water,

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.480
so and we know that we
have, in our product,

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.920
3.47 grams of water.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.530
3.47 grams of H2O,

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.640
and now let's think about how
many moles of H2O that is.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.250
So that's going to be, let's see,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.170
every one mole of H2O

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:39.400
is going to have a mass
of how many grams of H2O?

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:40.960
And we could do that up here.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.210
H2O, it's going to be,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.930
we have two hydrogens,

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:48.740
so it's going to be two times 1.008

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.310
plus the mass, the average atomic mass

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.760
of the oxygen is going to be plus 16,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.330
but we can also view that as

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.030
what would be the mass in grams,

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:00.280
if you had a mole of it?

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.280
And so this is going to
be in grams per mole,

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:04.770
and so this is going to be, let's see,

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.280
two times 1.008.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.623
This part over here is 2.016,

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:11.680
and then you add 16 to it.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:12.880
It's going to be 18.016.

00:07:14.711 --> 00:07:17.070
18.016,

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.580
and then if I just calculated this,

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.710
this would give me how
many moles of water I have

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.140
in my product,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:26.780
but I care about moles of hydrogen.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.560
And so how many moles
of hydrogen do I have

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.260
for every mole of water?

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.410
So for how many moles of hydrogen

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:38.853
for every mole of water?

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.520
Well I'm going to have
two moles of hydrogen

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.990
for every mole of water

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.020
because in each water molecule,

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.500
I have two hydrogens.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.317
And so that's going to
cancel out with that

00:07:50.317 --> 00:07:52.540
and we're just going to be
left with moles of hydrogen.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:56.300
And so this is going to be equal to,

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:01.300
I'll take my 3.47 grams of water,

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.970
divide it by the,

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:08.100
how much, how many grams a mole of water,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.260
what its mass would be,

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:11.417
so divided by 18.016.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.510
This is how many moles of water I have.

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:20.480
Now for every,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.440
for every molecule of
water, I have two hydrogens,

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.770
so then I will multiply by two.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:29.260
Times two is equal to zero point,

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.270
I'll just round three
digits right over here.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:32.693
0.385.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:39.020
0.385 moles of hydrogen.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.250
So now we know the
number of hydrogen atoms.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.420
We know the number of carbon atoms,

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.220
and to figure out the empirical
formula of the compound,

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.240
we can think about the
ratio between the two,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:55.240
so I'm gonna find the ratio
of hydrogens to carbons.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.970
And that is going to be equal to,

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:03.970
I have 0.385 moles of hydrogen

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.430
over, over

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.540
0.128 moles of carbon.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.940
0.128 moles of carbon,

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:14.750
and what is this equal to?

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.740
And it looks like it's
going to be roughly three,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.670
but let me verify that.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.050
In my head, that seems so,

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.670
I already have the hydrogen there,

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:27.670
and so if I divide it by 0.128,

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:30.960
I get, yep, pretty close to three.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.750
Now actually, I think if I, yep,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.550
pretty close to three, so there you go.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.880
This is approximately three,

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.800
and so I can, with pretty good confidence,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.830
this was very close to three.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.540
I could say for every carbon,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.630
I have three hydrogens
in my original compound,

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.150
in this thing right over here.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.060
So the empirical formula
of our original compound

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.780
for every one carbon,

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.200
I have three hydrogens.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.770
So CH3,

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:01.543
and we are done.

